<DocLayout
  title="Skills"
  description="SKILL.md packs, discovery, the composer picker, and how skills differ from MCP tools."
>
  <p>
    A <strong>skill</strong> is a <code>SKILL.md</code> pack: prompt text that
    teaches the assistant a procedure. Skills are discovered from the opened
    project, your user config directory, <code>~/.agents/skills</code>, and a
    bundled set. Enable or disable them, inspect their content, and enabled
    skills are injected into the chat system prompt.
  </p>

  <h2>Using skills</h2>
  <p>
    The composer's <strong>Skill</strong> chip attaches one skill to a single
    message, and keyword auto-suggest can propose one from your draft. The
    enabled skills in a message are listed in the reply so you can see exactly
    what the assistant was given.
  </p>

  <h2>How skills differ from MCP tools</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Skills</strong> change what the assistant <em>knows how to do</em> — instructions, injected as text.</li>
    <li><strong>MCP tools</strong> change what it <em>can call</em> — real capabilities, permission-gated.</li>
    <li><strong>Agents</strong> combine both inside the bounded run loop; see <a href={withBase('/docs/agents/')}>Agents &amp; runs</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Honest limits</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Skills are prompt text, not executed code — a skill cannot run anything by itself.</li>
    <li>There are no per-skill permissions because a skill cannot run anything on its own.</li>
  </ul>
</DocLayout>
